Fix a correlation defect in discver's output artifacts. Problem: findings have no stable unique identifier threaded across outputs. Two issues: (1) crash artifacts are named by the SHA-1 of the crashing INPUT, so distinct bugs triggered by the same input (e.g. two harnesses both crashing on empty input) collide to the same filename/hash — content-hash is not a crash identity. (2) There is no single primary key shared across the four artifact types: bug-candidate JSONs, crashes/<harness>/ dirs, unique_bugs/, and report.json — so correlating them requires inference.

Fix: assign every finding a STABLE, UNIQUE finding_id at creation (e.g. a uuid, or harness+sink+input-hash+counter — something that stays unique even when the input hash collides). Thread that finding_id through ALL emitted artifacts: the candidate JSON (as a field), the crash directory/files (in the record, not just the filename), unique_bugs entries, crash-summary, and report.json. Keep the existing input-hash too (it's still useful for dedup), but it must not be the sole identity. The goal: a downstream consumer joins every artifact on finding_id with zero inference.

Read how findings/crashes/candidates are currently created and written (crash_analyzer.py, crash_dedup.py, triage.py, report.py, and wherever crash dirs + bug-candidate JSONs are written). Make the id assignment happen once at the point a finding is first recorded, then flow through. Behind a flag (DISCVER_STABLE_IDS, default OFF) so current output is unchanged unless enabled. Add tests: two findings with identical input-hash get DISTINCT finding_ids; the same finding_id appears in candidate JSON, crash record, unique_bugs, and report.json for one finding. Verify with py_compile and tests. Update CHANGELOG/README. Report exactly which artifacts now carry finding_id and how a consumer joins on it. Commit.
